firstDraft.text:
Run both `step.waitForEvent` calls inside `group.parallel()`, which is the supported way to get a real race [1]. The default `Promise.race` waits for all parallel steps under optimized parallelism in v4 [1].

There is one catch. `waitForEvent` resolves to `null` on timeout instead of rejecting [2]. A bare race would therefore return `null` at 1000 ms, even if the other event arrives at 1100 ms. Fall back to the other result when the first one is null:

```ts
import { group } from "inngest";

const evt = await group.parallel(async () => {
  const uploaded = step.waitForEvent("wait-uploaded", {
    event: "stamp/uploaded",
    timeout: 1000,
  });
  const skipped = step.waitForEvent("wait-skipped", {
    event: "stamp/skipped",
    timeout: 1200,
  });

  const first = await Promise.race([uploaded, skipped]);
  if (first) return first;

  // The first to settle timed out, so wait for the other one
  const [u, s] = await Promise.all([uploaded, skipped]);
  return u ?? s ?? null;
});
```

`evt` is the first event to arrive, or `null` if neither arrives before the timeouts. The remaining step isn't cancelled when the race resolves early [1]. It will still run until it receives its event or times out.

I wrote this from memory of the `group.parallel` API and haven't run it. Check the `group` import path against your SDK version.

If you can use a single event name, such as `stamp/resolved` with a `data.status` field, one `waitForEvent` with an `if` expression is simpler.

