Two mostly separate things are going on.

**1. The 404s and "No routes matched location '/x/inngest'".** The dev server auto-discovers apps by polling common serve paths every few seconds: `/x/inngest`, `/api/inngest`, `/.netlify/functions/inngest` and `/.redwood/functions/inngest`. Your Remix app has no route at any of them, so you get the 404s and Remix's "No routes matched" warning. `npm i inngest` doesn't create that route. Add a resource route that exports both `loader` and `action` [9], for example `app/routes/api.inngest.ts`:

```ts
import { serve } from "inngest/remix";
import { inngest } from "~/inngest/client";
import fns from "~/inngest";

const handler = serve({ client: inngest, functions: fns });
export { handler as loader, handler as action };
```

Then run `npx inngest-cli@latest dev -u http://localhost:3000/api/inngest`. To stop the guessing, add `--no-discovery`. The `loader` is needed because Remix only routes GET requests to a loader [9]. The `serve` signature depends on your SDK version. The example above is v3 style.

**2. "Could not find an event key".** The SDK logs this when it sends an event with no `INNGEST_EVENT_KEY` and isn't in dev mode. Event keys aren't needed with the Dev Server [1][4]. Set `INNGEST_DEV=1` (or run with `NODE_ENV=development`) so the SDK uses dev mode. In production, set `INNGEST_EVENT_KEY` [1]. Also check where your code calls `inngest.send()`.

**3. The React `<title>` warning** isn't related to Inngest. Pass `<title>` a single string, e.g. `<title>{`${a} | ${b}`}</title>`.

control.text:
Two separate things are going on, and neither is a bug in the dev server itself.

**1. The 404s / "No routes matched location '/x/inngest'"**: The dev server auto-discovers your app by polling a list of common serve endpoints (`/api/inngest`, `/x/inngest`, `/.netlify/functions/inngest`, `/.redwood/functions/inngest`, etc.) every few seconds. Installing `inngest` isn't enough. Your Remix app doesn't have a serve endpoint yet, so every probe 404s. Remix logs the `/x/inngest` one as "No routes matched".

Fix: create a route that serves Inngest, e.g. `app/routes/api.inngest.ts`:

```ts
import { serve } from "inngest/remix";
import { inngest } from "~/inngest/client";
import { functions } from "~/inngest/functions";

const handler = serve({ client: inngest, functions });
export { handler as action, handler as loader };
```

Then point the dev server at it: `npx inngest-cli@latest dev -u http://localhost:3000/api/inngest` (or add the URL in the Dev Server UI under Apps). Note `/api/inngest` is a default probe path, so it may be found automatically once it exists.

**2. "Could not find an event key to send events"**: This comes from the SDK when something calls `inngest.send()` without an event key. In local dev, set `INNGEST_DEV=1` (or `isDev: true` in the client) so the SDK sends to the dev server and doesn't need a key. Make sure that env var is loaded in your Remix process. Production needs `INNGEST_EVENT_KEY`.

**3. React `<title>` warning**: This is unrelated to Inngest. It's a Remix/React issue where `<title>` gets an array of children, e.g. `<title>{a} - {b}</title>`. Use a single template string: ``<title>{`${a} - ${b}`}</title>``.

firstDraft.text:
Passing `fetch` explicitly is the supported way to handle this. The `Inngest` client accepts a `fetch` option, and that's the right fix on Edge when auto-detection picks the wrong implementation:

```ts
export const inngest = new Inngest({
  id: "my-app",
  fetch: globalThis.fetch.bind(globalThis),
});
```

I can't point to a specific inngest-js release or issue that changes the Edge detection. The cause is that the SDK checks for a global `fetch` at module load, and some bundled Edge builds don't expose it in a way the check recognizes. It then falls back to `cross-fetch`, which needs `XMLHttpRequest`. Before relying on the workaround being temporary, check that you're on the latest `inngest` version and search the inngest-js issues. If it still reproduces on latest, open an issue with a minimal Next.js Edge repro.

Don't confuse this with the exported `fetch` utility in the docs [1]. That utility makes HTTP calls inside functions durable. It doesn't affect which fetch the client uses to talk to Inngest.

withDocs.text:
Passing `fetch` to the client is the documented-style workaround, and I can't point to a specific inngest-js release or issue that changes Edge detection. The `Inngest` client does accept a `fetch` option:

```ts
export const inngest = new Inngest({
  id: "my-app",
