```

Why it happens: when the SDK doesn't find a usable global `fetch`, it falls back to a polyfill (`cross-fetch`), which relies on `XMLHttpRequest`. I can't confirm from the sources exactly why detection fails in your Edge bundle, so I wouldn't state a precise cause.

Next steps: upgrade to the latest `inngest` version, then search the inngest-js issues. If it still reproduces, open an issue with a minimal Next.js Edge repro. Until then, keep the explicit `fetch` option. It's safe to leave in place.

Don't confuse this with the exported `fetch` utility [1]. That makes HTTP calls inside functions durable. It doesn't change which fetch the client uses to talk to Inngest.

firstDraft.text:
Yes. A single function can have both cron and event triggers. Multiple triggers are supported (up to 10 unique ones) and you can mix events and crons in one function [1].

In current SDKs (v4), put them all in a `triggers` array:

```ts
inngest.createFunction(
  {
    id: "resync-user-data",
    triggers: [
      { event: "user.created" },
      { cron: "0 5 * * *" },
    ],
  },
  async ({ event, step }) => {
    // ...
  },
);
```

The likely cause of the error is passing a single trigger object that has both `event` and `cron` keys. A trigger object takes one or the other, so list them as separate entries. On older v3 SDKs, multi-trigger was added as an array in the second argument, for example `[{ event: "a" }, { cron: "..." }]` [8]. Check the SDK version if the array form doesn't type-check.

When the function runs, `event.name` tells you which trigger fired it. Cron runs get the `inngest/scheduled.timer` event [8]. Overlapping crons are de-duplicated, so only one run fires per second [4].

withDocs.text:
Yes. A single function can have both cron and event triggers. Inngest supports up to 10 unique triggers per function, and you can mix events and crons [1].

In current SDKs (v4), put them all in a `triggers` array:

```ts
inngest.createFunction(
  {
    id: "resync-user-data",
    triggers: [
      { event: "user.created" },
      { cron: "0 5 * * *" },
    ],
  },
  async ({ event, step }) => {
    // ...
  },
);
```

Each trigger is its own array entry. Don't put `event` and `cron` keys in the same object. On older SDKs, multiple triggers go in the second argument as an array, e.g. `[{ event: "a" }, { cron: "..." }]` [8]. If the array form doesn't type-check, check your SDK version.

Inside the handler, `event.name` tells you which trigger fired the run. Cron runs get the `inngest/scheduled.timer` event [8]. If several crons overlap, only one cron run fires per given second [4].

