Yes. Inngest has a **Priority** flow control feature. You set `priority.run` on the function to an expression that evaluates to an integer from -600 to 600 (seconds). A positive value makes the run behave as if it were enqueued that many seconds earlier, so it jumps ahead of other queued runs. The sources I was given don't cover it, so this comes from my knowledge of the feature. Check the Priority docs page for the exact syntax.

```ts
inngest.createFunction(
  {
    id: "generate-image",
    triggers: { event: "image/generate.requested" },
    priority: {
      // paid users jump ahead by up to 120s; heavy users get pushed back
      run: "event.data.tier == 'paid' ? 120 : 0 - event.data.recentUsage",
    },
    concurrency: { limit: 20 },
  },
  async ({ event, step }) => { /* ... */ }
);
```

**Usage-based deprioritization:** the expression can only read the triggering event's data. So compute the usage score (for example, recent job count clamped to the allowed range) when you send the event, and put it in `event.data`.

**Fairness within a tier:** add a per-user concurrency key. This stops one heavy user from filling the queue [1][3]:

```ts
concurrency: [{ key: "event.data.user_id", limit: 2 }]
```

Priority only reorders runs that are waiting in the queue. It only matters when you have a concurrency limit or other backlog. Without a limit, runs start immediately regardless of priority.

For hard tier separation, you can also use two functions with different concurrency limits, one for free and one for paid.

